  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1.",
   "Arduino Nano Matter was not found by lookup_part, so it is defined as a new part type.",
   "The Google Nest Hub Max (Thread Border Router) is network infrastructure, not part of the circuit, so it is left out.",
   "The tutorial says only: the driver is powered from 3.3V and the motor speed comes from a PWM signal on D4. I wired 3V3 to VCC, GND to GND and D4 to the driver's SDA line, since the module only has the I2C Grove pins GND/VCC/SDA/SCL. Which signal pin the D4 wire goes to is a guess.",
   "The photo shows a Grove cable and a green Grove breakout adapter between the board and the driver. The parts list has neither, so they are not included, and the jumper wires go straight to the driver ports.",
   "The Nano Matter sits on the breadboard with only 3V3, D4 and GND in holes. Hole positions (5t.a, 6t.a, 7t.a) are assumed, and each jumper wire's breadboard end is in the same column in row b.",
   "The motor's own leads go to OUT1/OUT2. Its polarity is not specified, and the motor is symmetric in the catalogue.",
   "The USB-C cable powers the board from the computer."
  ]
 },
